Hooks in job.c. A spawned child forgets its parent's children. The blocking wait answers
call requests, and an unknown child that dies is parked for the spawn api. A recursive
make that halts halts this one, and a halted process runs no more lines and leaves once
the recipe is collected. Every hook hears a recipe start and end. An imported target's job
is a fork of make that runs its engine, which stays here since it uses this file's child
setup, and gives back any pool token the engine still holds should it return. Each recipe
child gets its own call pair and its line's stdin pipe, and gives both back when it is
freed. A recipe target whose output is a kept value prints into its value file, and its
name's job prints that value like an imported target's.

